Subject: Regression of trait on autoregressive parameter
Message-ID: <Hy5fcB3zWKM.JInWFnEeBwAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/Hy5fcB3zWKM/m/JInWFnEeBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

 I am interested in the influence of a trait (tsc; trait self-control) on the stability of a repeatedly measured variable in a random-intercept autoregressive model  <https://www.researchgate.net/publication/341955022_Multilevel_Autoregressive_Models_when_the_Number_of_Time_Points_is_Small>(i.e., the orange arrows):

The original syntax (without the orange paths) is:

auto_ep <- "
# random effects
ri =~ 1* ep_t2 + 1* ep_t3 + 1* ep_t4
ri ~~ vari *ri + delta *ep_t1
# mean structure
ep_t1 ~ alpha0 *1
ep_t2 ~ alpha *1 + rho *ep_t1
ep_t3 ~ alpha *1 + rho *ep_t2
ep_t4 ~ alpha *1 + rho *ep_t3
# residual coepriance model
ep_t1 ~~ resepr0 *ep_t1
ep_t2 ~~ resepr *ep_t2
ep_t3 ~~ resepr *ep_t3
ep_t4 ~~ resepr *ep_t4
ri ~ tsc rs ~ tsc"

It seems easy to regress tsc on eta ("ri") by adding "ri ~ tsc".

But, adding a regression on the autoregressive parameter ("rho", by "rho ~ tsc") results in the error message: “lavaan ERROR: missing observed variables in dataset: rho”.

Well, it seems problematic to regress a trait on an individual parameter. However, I am interested in the effect of tsc on the stability coefficient, expecting high tsc being associated with more stability.

